---
import ImportFlow from "../components/ImportFlow.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../lib/site";

// A first run, shortened from the sample run in docs/first-books.md: what you
// type, the agent's plan, a progress line, its one round of questions, and the
// link it ends on. Keep the agent's lines under 60 characters, because they
// keep their columns and don't wrap.
type RunTurn = { from: "you" | "agent" | "progress"; text: string };
const run: RunTurn[] = [
  { from: "you", text: "Do my books from the statements in ~/Downloads/statements" },
  { from: "agent", text: `I read 4 files. They are these accounts:

  Standard Chartered  savings account  Oct 2026
  Federal Bank        savings account  Oct 2026
  HDFC Millennia      credit card      Oct 2026
  StanC Super Value   credit card      Oct 2026

This will take a few minutes. You can leave it running.
Go ahead?` },
  { from: "you", text: "go" },
  { from: "progress", text: "Imported Standard Chartered: 41 transactions, 38 sorted" },
  { from: "agent", text: `Need your call
  1  ACH D- HDFC BANK CAR LOAN EMI (1 · 10,000)
       a) HDFC Car Loan    b) Loan Interest   c) skip
  2  IMPS CR/SAMPLE SENDER (1 · 1,500)
       a) Gifts Received   b) Receivables     c) skip` },
  { from: "you", text: "1a 2a" },
  { from: "agent", text: `Your books are ready to review:
http://localhost:2345/review` },
];

// The rules file a user ends up with after some months, shown as the text
// file it is (custom_mappings_default.prompt): the user's own rules, added as
// their statements turned up what only they know (who the landlord is, who
// owes them), then after a blank line a few of the starter chart's notes.
// Each is written as the kind of transaction, then an arrow, then its
// account, so a skimmer reads it as a mapping. The user's rules are illustrative, not from the demo books.
// One rule per line; the editor wraps them, and an empty string is a blank line.
const rulesFile = "custom_mappings_default.prompt";
const rules = [
  "- IMPS of 30,000–35,000 to A/c XX4417 in the first week of the month → Rent",
  "- NEFT from ACME TECHNOLOGIES on the last working day → Salary; anything else from them → Reimbursements",
  "- UPI from Arjun → Loan to Arjun (he's paying back what I lent him in March)",
  "- Amazon under 1,000 → Household Supplies; over that, ask me (it's usually a gift or a gadget)",
  "",
  "- Meals from food delivery apps → Food Delivery",
  "- Restaurants, cafés, bakeries and bars → Dining Out",
  "- Bank and card fees, and the tax on them → Bank Charges",
];
// A rule's parts, for highlighting: an arrow and the account it maps to
// (up to a ";", a "(" or the end), a note in parentheses, and plain text
// between them.
type RulePart = { kind: "text" | "account" | "note"; text: string };
function ruleParts(rule: string): RulePart[] {
  const parts: RulePart[] = [];
  let last = 0;
  for (const match of rule.matchAll(/\([^)]*\)|→ [^;(]*[^;(\s]/g)) {
    if (match.index > last) parts.push({ kind: "text", text: rule.slice(last, match.index) });
    parts.push({ kind: match[0].startsWith("(") ? "note" : "account", text: match[0] });
    last = match.index + match[0].length;
  }
  if (last < rule.length) parts.push({ kind: "text", text: rule.slice(last) });
  return parts;
}
---
<SiteLayout title={site.title} description={site.description} path="/">
  <!--
    Flow: each step answers the question the last one raises, and the H2s
    alone tell the story.
    1. Hero: what it is, in the headline, and what you do with it, under it;
       Get started, with a prompt beside it that you paste into a coding
       agent to install dbu6, and under them that it's free and open source
       with no tracking or telemetry; and the Overview it gives you, with
       the demo (in a lightbox) played from it.
       The subtitle, the app at localhost, and the window bars' paths in
       ~/my-books show that the books are local.
    2. Automatic bookkeeping with your coding agent (#agent): the agents it works with and
       what you can ask of yours, with importing statements, beside a sample
       run; then two questions asked of it, which switch between their
       answers, beside the chosen answer.
    3. Double-entry bookkeeping (#trust, #reports): the system professional
       accountants use, and the whole picture it gives you, in four reports:
       income and expenses, the balance sheet, net worth over time, and
       every account's ledger, as four tabs over one screenshot.
    4. Import statements and categorize transactions automatically (#how):
       the header, lede and uploading statements beside an illustration of
       the agent writing a parser for a statement, then categorizing them
       beside the rules file.
    5. Questions (#faq), a table of questions and answers on the desk
       engraving.

    Automatic bookkeeping with your coding agent sits on a white band, so the move from
    the hero's centred copy to the left-aligned chapters reads as a new
    chapter; from Double-entry on, sections alternate between a soft band
    and the page. The look (the desk surface, the cards, the engraving
    at the foot) is homepage.css's.

    Voice: calm, positive, and moving forward. Say what the reader gets and
    does, not what goes wrong without it.

    Screenshots live in public/assets/. Each img alt is a description, then
    "Capture: …" with everything needed to re-take it. Keep file names when
    re-taking them.

    App screens: a throwaway project linked to the dbu6 checkout ("@dbu6/app":
    "link:…/dbu6", as DEVELOPMENT.md describes), `dbu6 seed` run on 2026-10-27,
    signed in as the seed's demo user; viewport 1440×900 @2x, light; each clip
    box is in CSS pixels. Re-take them when the app or the sample data changes.

    Agent screens: frames of the real Claude Code sessions recorded for the
    video, in dbu6-videos/teaser2/screen-recordings/ (see its
    screen-recordings.md), cropped to the answer. They don't depend on dbu6's
    screens, only on the demo books the sessions read.
  -->
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="container header-inner">
      <a class="site-name" href="/">dbu6</a>
      <div class="header-links">
        <a href="/docs/">Documentation</a>
        <a href={site.github}><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 0C3.58 0 0 3.58 0 8c0 3.54 2.29 6.53 5.47 7.59.4.07.55-.17.55-.38 0-.19-.01-.82-.01-1.49-2.01.37-2.53-.49-2.69-.94-.09-.23-.48-.94-.82-1.13-.28-.15-.68-.52-.01-.53.63-.01 1.08.58 1.23.82.72 1.21 1.87.87 2.33.66.07-.52.28-.87.51-1.07-1.78-.2-3.64-.89-3.64-3.95 0-.87.31-1.59.82-2.15-.08-.2-.36-1.02.08-2.12 0 0 .67-.21 2.2.82.64-.18 1.32-.27 2-.27.68 0 1.36.09 2 .27 1.53-1.04 2.2-.82 2.2-.82.44 1.1.16 1.92.08 2.12.51.56.82 1.27.82 2.15 0 3.07-1.87 3.75-3.65 3.95.29.25.54.73.54 1.48 0 1.07-.01 1.93-.01 2.2 0 .21.15.46.55.38A8.013 8.013 0 0016 8c0-4.42-3.58-8-8-8z"/></svg>GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section class="section hero">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <!-- Two columns above the Overview: on the left, the headline, what
               you get and do with it, Get started and the install prompt, and
               under them the coding agents that drive it and that it's free
               and open source with no tracking or telemetry; on the right,
               the three outcome pillars (any bank or format, almost no manual
               work, and professional double-entry books). -->
          <div class="hero-main">
            <h1>AI workspace for your personal finance</h1>
            <p class="hero-subtitle"><strong>Clear, complete books for your money.</strong> Track your income and expenses, see your net worth, and use your coding agent to answer any question about your spending, plan for major life decisions, or build custom reports.</p>
            <div class="actions">
              <a class="btn-primary btn-hero" href="/docs/install/">Get started<svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 8h9.5M8.5 4l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a>
              <div class="agent-prompt">
                <span class="agent-prompt-label">Paste into your agent</span>
                <span class="agent-prompt-line">
                  <span class="agent-prompt-text">Install dbu6 by following dbu6.com/docs/install.md</span>
                  <button type="button" class="copy" data-copy="Install dbu6 by following https://dbu6.com/docs/install.md" aria-label="Copy the prompt" hidden><svg class="icon-copy" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5.5 5.5h8v8h-8z M10.5 5.5v-3h-8v8h3"/></svg><svg class="icon-copied" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 8.5 6.5 12 13 4.5"/></svg></button>
                </span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <dl class="hero-facts">
              <div>
                <dt>Works with</dt>
                <dd>Claude Code, Codex, Pi</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>License</dt>
                <dd><a href={site.github}>Free &amp; open source (MIT)</a></dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Privacy</dt>
                <dd>No tracking · No telemetry</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>

          <ul class="points hero-points">
            <li>
              <h4>Works with any bank, in any format</h4>
              <p>Drop PDFs, Excel, or CSV statements from any institution—no brittle third-party bank syncs to set up or maintain.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h4>Automatic transaction categorization</h4>
              <p>Every transaction is automatically sorted into the right account—like Subscriptions, Groceries, or Dining Out. Answer a few simple questions when something ambiguous comes up, and your agent remembers your rules from then on.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h4>Accountant-grade double-entry books</h4>
              <p>The same accounting system CAs, CPAs, and every business—small and large—rely on around the world.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h4>Stored locally in SQLite</h4>
              <p>Your books live in a single portable, scriptable SQLite file on your machine that you and your agent can query and build on directly.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <!-- The app in a browser window at the address `dbu6 dev` serves it
             on, so the page shows where it runs. Watch the demo sits on it,
             as a play button over a video's first frame.
             Watch the demo is hidden until there's a better video; to bring
             it back, uncomment the link below. The demo dialog and its
             script stay in place, so nothing else needs to change. -->
        <figure class="shot showcase">
          <div class="browser">
            <div class="browser-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="window-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span class="address">localhost:2345</span></div>
            {/* <a class="play" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0PsCb0-01dI" data-demo><span class="play-mark"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 2.5v11l9.5-5.5z"/></svg></span>Watch the demo</a> */}
            <a href="/assets/overview.png"><img src="/assets/overview.png" srcset="/assets/overview.png 2x" alt="dbu6's Overview for September 2026: drafts waiting for review; income, spending, savings and savings rate, each with the months before it; spending by category with each category's months before it; net worth as assets less liabilities; and each account's balance and the date its books run to. Capture: seed to 2026-11-01; clock 2026-11-01; open /?month=2026-09; clip x316 y0 w1020 h745."></a>
          </div>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <!-- The demo, opened over the page by the Watch the demo button. The
           player loads only when it opens, and unloads when it closes. -->
      <dialog class="demo-dialog" id="demo" aria-label="dbu6 demo">
        <form method="dialog"><button type="submit" class="dialog-close">Close</button></form>
        <figure class="video">
          <div class="frame">
            <iframe title="dbu6 demo" data-src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/0PsCb0-01dI?autoplay=1"
              allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
              referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
          </div>
        </figure>
      </dialog>

      <!-- A screenshot, opened over the page by clicking it and fitted to
           the lightbox. -->
      <dialog class="shot-dialog" id="screenshot" aria-label="Screenshot">
        <form method="dialog"><button type="submit" class="dialog-close">Close</button></form>
        <div class="shot-view"><img alt=""></div>
      </dialog>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="agent">
      <div class="container">
        <!-- The heading and what the agent does for you, beside a sample run.
             The run is tall and narrow, so it sits beside the copy, from the
             top of the section. -->
        <div class="agent-row">
          <div class="agent-copy">
            <h2>Automatic bookkeeping with your coding agent</h2>
            <!-- The marks are Simple Icons' Claude and OpenAI paths; Pi has no
                 mark of its own, so it gets the letter. -->
            <ul class="chips agent-chips" aria-label="Coding agents">
              <li><svg class="mark" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m4.7144 15.9555 4.7174-2.6471.079-.2307-.079-.1275h-.2307l-.7893-.0486-2.6956-.0729-2.3375-.0971-2.2646-.1214-.5707-.1215-.5343-.7042.0546-.3522.4797-.3218.686.0608 1.5179.1032 2.2767.1578 1.6514.0972 2.4468.255h.3886l.0546-.1579-.1336-.0971-.1032-.0972L6.973 9.8356l-2.55-1.6879-1.3356-.9714-.7225-.4918-.3643-.4614-.1578-1.0078.6557-.7225.8803.0607.2246.0607.8925.686 1.9064 1.4754 2.4893 1.8336.3643.3035.1457-.1032.0182-.0728-.164-.2733-1.3539-2.4467-1.445-2.4893-.6435-1.032-.17-.6194c-.0607-.255-.1032-.4674-.1032-.7285L6.287.1335 6.6997 0l.9957.1336.419.3642.6192 1.4147 1.0018 2.2282 1.5543 3.0296.4553.8985.2429.8318.091.255h.1579v-.1457l.1275-1.706.2368-2.0947.2307-2.6957.0789-.7589.3764-.9107.7468-.4918.5828.2793.4797.686-.0668.4433-.2853 1.8517-.5586 2.9021-.3643 1.9429h.2125l.2429-.2429.9835-1.3053 1.6514-2.0643.7286-.8196.85-.9046.5464-.4311h1.0321l.759 1.1293-.34 1.1657-1.0625 1.3478-.8804 1.1414-1.2628 1.7-.7893 1.36.0729.1093.1882-.0183 2.8535-.607 1.5421-.2794 1.8396-.3157.8318.3886.091.3946-.3278.8075-1.967.4857-2.3072.4614-3.4364.8136-.0425.0304.0486.0607 1.5482.1457.6618.0364h1.621l3.0175.2247.7892.522.4736.6376-.079.4857-1.2142.6193-1.6393-.3886-3.825-.9107-1.3113-.3279h-.1822v.1093l1.0929 1.0686 2.0035 1.8092 2.5075 2.3314.1275.5768-.3218.4554-.34-.0486-2.2039-1.6575-.85-.7468-1.9246-1.621h-.1275v.17l.4432.6496 2.3436 3.5214.1214 1.0807-.17.3521-.6071.2125-.6679-.1214-1.3721-1.9246L14.38 17.959l-1.1414-1.9428-.1397.079-.674 7.2552-.3156.3703-.7286.2793-.6071-.4614-.3218-.7468.3218-1.4753.3886-1.9246.3157-1.53.2853-1.9004.17-.6314-.0121-.0425-.1397.0182-1.4328 1.9672-2.1796 2.9446-1.7243 1.8456-.4128.164-.7164-.3704.0667-.6618.4008-.5889 2.386-3.0357 1.4389-1.882.929-1.0868-.0062-.1579h-.0546l-6.3385 4.1164-1.1293.1457-.4857-.4554.0608-.7467.2307-.2429 1.9064-1.3114Z"/></svg>Claude Code</li>
              <li><svg class="mark" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22.2819 9.8211a5.9847 5.9847 0 0 0-.5157-4.9108 6.0462 6.0462 0 0 0-6.5098-2.9A6.0651 6.0651 0 0 0 4.9807 4.1818a5.9847 5.9847 0 0 0-3.9977 2.9 6.0462 6.0462 0 0 0 .7427 7.0966 5.98 5.98 0 0 0 .511 4.9107 6.051 6.051 0 0 0 6.5146 2.9001A5.9847 5.9847 0 0 0 13.2599 24a6.0557 6.0557 0 0 0 5.7718-4.2058 5.9894 5.9894 0 0 0 3.9977-2.9001 6.0557 6.0557 0 0 0-.7475-7.0729zm-9.022 12.6081a4.4755 4.4755 0 0 1-2.8764-1.0408l.1419-.0804 4.7783-2.7582a.7948.7948 0 0 0 .3927-.6813v-6.7369l2.02 1.1686a.071.071 0 0 1 .038.052v5.5826a4.504 4.504 0 0 1-4.4945 4.4944zm-9.6607-4.1254a4.4708 4.4708 0 0 1-.5346-3.0137l.142.0852 4.783 2.7582a.7712.7712 0 0 0 .7806 0l5.8428-3.3685v2.3324a.0804.0804 0 0 1-.0332.0615L9.74 19.9502a4.4992 4.4992 0 0 1-6.1408-1.6464zM2.3408 7.8956a4.485 4.485 0 0 1 2.3655-1.9728V11.6a.7664.7664 0 0 0 .3879.6765l5.8144 3.3543-2.0201 1.1685a.0757.0757 0 0 1-.071 0l-4.8303-2.7865A4.504 4.504 0 0 1 2.3408 7.872zm16.5963 3.8558L13.1038 8.364 15.1192 7.2a.0757.0757 0 0 1 .071 0l4.8303 2.7913a4.4944 4.4944 0 0 1-.6765 8.1042v-5.6772a.79.79 0 0 0-.407-.667zm2.0107-3.0231l-.142-.0852-4.7735-2.7818a.7759.7759 0 0 0-.7854 0L9.409 9.2297V6.8974a.0662.0662 0 0 1 .0284-.0615l4.8303-2.7866a4.4992 4.4992 0 0 1 6.6802 4.66zM8.3065 12.863l-2.02-1.1638a.0804.0804 0 0 1-.038-.0567V6.0742a4.4992 4.4992 0 0 1 7.3757-3.4537l-.142.0805L8.704 5.459a.7948.7948 0 0 0-.3927.6813zm1.0976-2.3654l2.602-1.4998 2.6069 1.4998v2.9994l-2.5974 1.4997-2.6067-1.4997Z"/></svg>Codex</li>
              <li><svg class="pi" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2.5 4.5h11 M5.75 4.5v9 M10.25 4.5v7.25a1.75 1.75 0 0 0 3 1.25"/></svg>Pi</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="agent-lede">You can run dbu6 entirely from your agent, without ever opening the app. Ask it to import your statements, get financial projections, or create new reports and features.</p>
            <h3>Import your statements with one prompt</h3>
            <p>Point your agent at a folder of bank and card statements. It identifies which account each file belongs to, imports and categorizes the transactions, and verifies the totals against each statement's closing balance. When a transaction is ambiguous—such as a transfer to a person it hasn't seen before—it asks you how to classify it.</p>
          </div>

          <!-- A sample run: what you type in the grey bubble, as on the
               answers, and the agent's messages as the aligned text it
               prints. -->
          <figure class="ask agent-run" aria-label="A sample first run with the coding agent">
            <figcaption><span class="ask-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="window-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>Do my books<span class="ask-folder">~/my-books</span></span></figcaption>
            <div class="run-turns">
              {run.map((turn) => turn.from === "you"
                ? <p class="ask-question">{turn.text}</p>
                : <pre class:list={[{ "run-progress": turn.from === "progress" }]}>{turn.text}</pre>)}
            </div>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <!-- Each question is word for word what was typed to the agent in the
             take its answer is from (teaser2's screen recordings), and the
             session title is the one the app gave it. Each card is drawn as
             that session's window: the title bar (with the books folder the
             session runs in at its right), the question in the grey
             bubble the app shows what you typed in, and the answer under it,
             cropped from the recording to the answer alone.
             The questions, under the copy, switch between the answers, with
             a dot for each: each a short form of what was asked, and one
             answer shows at a time. -->
        <div class="agent-row asks" data-tabs>
          <div class="agent-copy">
            <h3>Ask anything about your money</h3>
            <p>Your agent can query your full history across bank accounts, cards, loans, and investments, and it already knows how taxes, interest rates, and compounding work. So it can answer specific questions about your past spending, run projections, or model a major financial decision using your actual numbers.</p>
            <div class="ask-cards" role="tablist" aria-label="Questions asked of the agent">
              <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-subscriptions" aria-controls="panel-subscriptions" aria-selected="true">
                <span class="report-name">List all my recurring subscriptions</span>
              </button>
              <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-afford" aria-controls="panel-afford" aria-selected="false">
                <span class="report-name">Is buying an apartment now a good idea?</span>
              </button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="shot ask" role="tabpanel" id="panel-subscriptions" aria-labelledby="tab-subscriptions">
            <figcaption><span class="ask-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="window-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>Recurring subscriptions and spending<span class="ask-folder">~/my-books</span></span><span class="ask-question">List all my recurring subscriptions and how much I've paid for them</span></figcaption>
            <a href="/assets/agent-subscriptions-answer.png"><img src="/assets/agent-subscriptions-answer.png" srcset="/assets/agent-subscriptions-answer.png 2x" alt="The agent's answer: you have 6 subscriptions and paid 38,748 for them from September 2025 to August 2026, in a table of each subscription, how often it charges, each charge, the number of charges and the total paid. Capture: frame at 85 s of subscriptions.mov, cropped x780 y740 w2270 h940 (device px), the scroll-down button painted out."></a>
          </figure>
          <figure class="shot ask" role="tabpanel" id="panel-afford" aria-labelledby="tab-afford">
            <figcaption><span class="ask-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="window-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>Bengaluru 2BHK affordability<span class="ask-folder">~/my-books</span></span><span class="ask-question">We're looking at a 2BHK in Bengaluru for about 90 lakh. Can we afford it? And if not, what could we afford, and when?</span></figcaption>
            <a href="/assets/agent-afford-answer.png"><img src="/assets/agent-afford-answer.png" srcset="/assets/agent-afford-answer.png 2x" alt="The agent's answer: short answer, not yet; the 90 lakh flat becomes doable around mid-2029 using EPF or late 2030 without, and 30 to 35 lakh is realistic today; the figures are from the books for September 2025 to August 2026; and what a 90 lakh flat needs, cash up front of about 37 lakh and an EMI of about 58,600 a month. Capture: frame at 171 s of afford-flat-2.mov, cropped x780 y860 w2270 h810 (device px), the scroll-down button painted out."></a>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section double-entry" id="trust">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="de-header">
          <h2>Double-entry bookkeeping</h2>
          <p class="de-lede">
            dbu6 is a complete accounting package, not just an expense tracker. So you get the same kind of insights and reports that were once limited to businesses employing professional accountants.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="reports" id="reports" data-tabs>
          <!-- Each tab is one block of text: the report's name and what it
               shows. The name alone labels the tab; the note describes it. -->
          <div class="report-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Reports">
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-income-expenses" aria-controls="panel-income-expenses" aria-selected="true" aria-labelledby="name-income-expenses" aria-describedby="note-income-expenses">
              <span class="report-name" id="name-income-expenses">Income and expenses</span>
              <span class="report-note" id="note-income-expenses">Across all banks and cards</span>
            </button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-balance-sheet" aria-controls="panel-balance-sheet" aria-selected="false" aria-labelledby="name-balance-sheet" aria-describedby="note-balance-sheet">
              <span class="report-name" id="name-balance-sheet">Balance sheet</span>
