    <section class="section import" id="how">
      <div class="container">
        <!-- The heading, then uploading statements under it, beside the
             illustration of the agent writing a parser for a statement; then
             categorizing them, beside the rules file. Each part is a heading,
             what it takes in (file chips) or sorts into (round account pills,
             each with its colour dot), and its points, each a small heading
             over a sentence or two. -->
        <div class="import-intro">
          <div class="import-header">
            <h2>Import statements and categorize transactions automatically</h2>
            <p class="lede">Skip manual data entry and brittle third-party bank syncs. Upload your monthly bank and card statements directly to dbu6, and let your coding agent parse the files and map every transaction to the right account in your books.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="import-copy">
            <h3>Upload statements from any bank, in any format</h3>
            <ul class="chips" aria-label="Statement formats">
              {["PDF", "Excel", "CSV", "TSV", "Text"].map((format) => (
                <li><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 1.5h5.5L13 5v9.5H4z M9.5 1.5V5H13"/></svg>{format}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <ul class="points">
              <li><h4>Generate bank parsers automatically</h4><p>The first time you upload an unfamiliar statement format, your agent writes and saves a local parser for that bank. Future statements parse instantly on your machine.</p></li>
              <li><h4>Review imports as drafts first</h4><p>New imports land in a staging table as drafts, so you can verify everything before committing transactions to your books.</p></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <ImportFlow />
        </div>

        <div class="import-row">
          <div class="import-copy">
            <h3>Categorize your transactions automatically</h3>
            <ul class="chips accounts" aria-label="Some of the accounts in a chart of accounts">
              <li style="--dot: #2b6a4b">Groceries</li>
              <li style="--dot: #4f5b9c">Subscriptions</li>
              <li style="--dot: #2f7480">Hotels</li>
              <li style="--dot: #c2410c">Dining Out</li>
            </ul>
            <ul class="points">
              <li><h4>Exact string matching</h4><p>Transactions are classified by first applying local string-matching rules that you define. For example, you can write a rule to match any transaction containing <code>"ANTHROPIC"</code> to <code>SaaS Expenses</code>.</p></li>
              <li><h4>Powerful plain English rules</h4><p>For everything else, your agent evaluates the transaction against your plain-English instructions. If there is any ambiguity, the agent asks you how to categorize it.</p></li>
              <li><h4>Grow your own system</h4><p>When you answer a question about a recurring or generalizable expense, your agent writes a new rule to your config. Over time, your ruleset grows tailored to your finances—and the agent rarely needs your intervention.</p></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <!-- The rules file open in a plain text editor: the window's
               title bar with the file's name, the lines with each
               rule's arrow and account in the accent and its note in grey,
               and a status bar saying what kind of file it is. -->
          <figure class="text-file" aria-label="Sample categorization rules">
            <figcaption><span class="window-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>~/my-books/user-config/<wbr>{rulesFile}</figcaption>
            <pre>{rules.map((rule) => (
              <span class="line">{rule ? ruleParts(rule).map((part) =>
                part.kind === "account" ? <><span class="arrow">→</span> <span class="account">{part.text.slice(2)}</span></>
                : part.kind === "note" ? <span class="note">{part.text}</span>
                : part.text
              ) : " "}</span>
            ))}</pre>
            <div class="editor-status" aria-hidden="true"><span>Plain Text</span></div>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="faq">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2>Questions</h2>
        </div>
        <!-- A table of questions and answers: each question beside its
             answer, and over it on a narrow screen. -->
        <dl class="faq">
          <div>
            <dt>How much work is it each month?</dt>
            <dd>
              <p>Very little. You don't enter transactions by hand. Once a month, you get your bank and card statements and point your coding agent at them (or upload them in the app). You may need to answer a few questions about transactions the agent can't confidently categorize.</p>
              <p>dbu6 also checks your books for problems such as duplicate entries and balance mismatches. Your agent can fix any of them in one click.</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What do I need to run it?</dt>
            <dd>
              <p>A Mac or Linux machine with Node.js 22.18 or newer, <code>uv</code> and <code>poppler</code> for reading PDF statements, and Claude Code, Codex, or Pi. See the <a href="/docs/install/">installation guide</a> for details.</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Where does my data live, and is it private?</dt>
            <dd>
              <p>Your books are a SQLite file in a folder on your computer, and dbu6 has no hosted service or cloud sync. Importing statements and applying your text rules happen locally, without the agent.</p>
              <p>However, when your coding agent reads your transactions (to write a parser for a new bank statement, or to categorize transactions, or to answer a question), that text and related data goes to your model provider under your plan.</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What if I need absolute privacy?</dt>
            <dd>
              <p>You can run a local model on your own computer, so your transactions never leave it. Pi can use models served by a local runner such as <a href="https://ollama.com/">Ollama</a>, and dbu6 uses whichever model you set as Pi's default.</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What does it cost?</dt>
            <dd>
              <p>dbu6 is free and open source (MIT). The agent runs on your local Claude Code, Codex, or Pi CLI.</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Will it work with my bank?</dt>
            <dd>
              <p>Yes. For any bank or card, your agent writes a reader from a sample statement and tests it. It's saved in your folder and used again on every later import.</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Do I need to know accounting?</dt>
            <dd>
              <p>No. Thanks to AI, you can now get the reporting power of double-entry bookkeeping without any of the learning curve.</p>
              <p>The only complexity with double-entry accounting was that you had to understand how to map a transaction into its style - which account to debit, which to credit, and how to structure the entries. All of these are now handled in dbu6 with the help of your coding agent. You can even write your transactions in freeform English, and your agent will follow the double-entry conventions in <code>AGENTS.md</code> to create them accurately.</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Can I export my books?</dt>
            <dd>
              <p>Yes. You can export your entire books to <a href="https://hledger.org/">hledger</a> in one click. They are plain-text files that are extremely portable and are supported by the excellent hledger ecosystem.</p>
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <!-- Each line has its mark: a scale for the license, GitHub's own
             mark, and a person for the maintainer. -->
